[125.291, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Because you said to go ahead without stopping, I'm treating this restatement as confirmed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   An original puzzle game, playable in the browser, as a single web page.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - Puzzle game: 15–20 curated levels in chapters, each chapter adding one idea that is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     needed to solve its levels. Pars equal the solver's optimal move counts.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Original: one core mechanic that is new, not a Sokoban reskin. Someone playing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     the first three levels should feel they learned something.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Playable in the browser: works on phone and desktop, with keyboard, swipe and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     on-screen buttons.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Single web page: still one self-contained HTML file with no external assets.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       A shareable puzzle game is judged on:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - how well it teaches, with no text walls;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - feel: smooth movement, sound, a satisfying win, and undo that is instant;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - a cohesive look (palette, type, layout) that doesn't look like default HTML;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - fairness: no dead-end levels and no padding;\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - a title screen and level select that make it feel finished.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   People would pick it over alternatives for the mechanic and for the \"aha\" moments.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       A finished-feeling game you can post on itch.io or a portfolio. It has a title\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   screen, a level-select map with stars against par, and a share button that copies\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   your progress, e.g. \"Mirror Twins: 14/20 levels, 11 stars\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: - 15–20 levels, each solver-verified, and each chapter's new idea shown to be required.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - The mirrored-twins core is kept, and a distinct original mechanic is added\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     on top, introduced within levels 1–3.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Polished look and feel on phone and desktop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Custom-level sharing only if it comes cheap.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       1. Solver checks: every level is solvable, par equals the optimum, and treating\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      each new element as plain floor changes or breaks the solution.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   2. A real Chrome run that plays every level's solution through actual keyboard\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      input, with phone- and desktop-size screenshots and zero console errors.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   Both are compared against the first version (11 levels, no title screen, no stars).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   Design reference only: Baba Is You, Stephen's Sausage Roll, Patrick's Parabox.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   No human playtest, because you can't run one now.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: Nothing new. Chrome is installed, and I confirmed that headless Chrome driven by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   puppeteer-core (in `.ambition/tools/`) loads the game and reads the page.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · you answered (all four questions) · no per-step review\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Step report**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Did:** I asked the altitude questions one at a time, restated the goal, confirmed I can run the real-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  browser proof, and saved the current game to `.ambition/steps/2/`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Found:** The first version has no title screen, no stars or par, and no distinct mechanic beyond the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  mirror. Those are the gaps to close. Headless Chrome works, so the browser proof is possible.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Changed course:** The goal is now a polished, shareable game rather than a bare prototype. Your \"one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  original mechanic\" target is the hardest requirement, and it will drive the next steps.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[125.423, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Step 3 of pushing the work further: Depth. Open your first message with this line on its own,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[33m  exactly: `▸ Step 3/8 · Depth`. Write that line nowhere else.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +15 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[175.212, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 3/8 · Depth\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What a top puzzle designer would do that wasn't asked for**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Make the mechanic algebraic, so the solver can prove that each new element is needed. A good puzzle's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  trick should be the only way through, not one of several.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Choose each level for how tight it is, not just for being solvable. I'd pick the one with the fewest\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  optimal solutions and the fewest objects.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Show the current rule on screen, so the player never has to remember hidden state.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Rawest material**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The real state space, searched exhaustively. Each state is both twins' positions plus the mirror rule,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  searched breadth-first with shortest-path counts. I'd use this instead of judging levels by eye or by random\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  generation alone.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Methods and instruments**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Original mechanic: flip pads.** The mirror is no longer fixed. The second twin moves as `(sx·dx,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sy·dy)`. It starts mirrored (`sx=-1`, `sy=1`). Stepping onto an `f` pad flips `sx`, and stepping onto a `g`\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pad flips `sy`. So the rule your own movement keeps changing is part of the puzzle.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Level generation.** A BFS solver with path counting searches for levels. Each level's chapter\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  requirement has to hold: without the new element (pads treated as floor), it must be unsolvable or shorter.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Then I pick the tightest candidate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **One source of truth.** One `logic.js` is inlined into the page, so the solver and the game can't drift\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  apart.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Real-browser proof.** Headless Chrome via puppeteer-core plays each optimal solution through real\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  keystrokes. It also takes phone and desktop screenshots.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Game feel.** Smooth sliding animation, WebAudio blips, a win burst, a title screen, a chapter map with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  stars, and a share button.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What else shows it's right:** the exhaustive solver and the real Chrome playthrough of every level.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Neither is me grading my own work.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Rewritten task:** build the flip-pad mirror game with about 16 solver-verified levels in four chapters\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  (Mirror, Flip, Pits, Both axes). Pick the tightest generated levels, make the page polished, and prove it\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with the solver and a full Chrome playthrough.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
