[874.779, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step report**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Target:** your fallback was \"levels that aren't padded\". I raised it to a measurable bar: every wall,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pad and pit in every level must be load-bearing. Removing any single object has to change the optimal\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  solution, either its length or how many optimal solutions there are. That is the discipline of tight puzzle\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  games such as Stephen's Sausage Roll.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Did:** I wrote `prune.js` to measure this and pruned redundant objects. It removes an object only if par\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  doesn't change, the optimal solutions don't multiply, and the chapter's teaching requirement still holds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Found:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The levels were already tight, with 66 of 73 objects load-bearing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The pruning removed 9 objects in total, leaving **64 of 64 load-bearing**. Every par is unchanged, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  level 15 went from two optimal solutions to one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The acceptance test still passes 19/19, with a real Chrome playthrough of all 16 levels.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - In the contact sheet the pruned levels look clean and sparse, with no clutter.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Where it fell short:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Pruning doesn't fix soft-locks. Level 10 still has 85 of its 155 reachable states unwinnable, and level\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  11 went from 13 to 47. The stuck warning and undo handle this, but redesigning those levels would take new\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  generation, which I didn't do.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - \"Load-bearing\" proves a level has no filler. It doesn't prove a level is fun. That is still a human\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  judgment I can't make, and your fresh-eyes playtest is still the real test.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Changed course:** none, and the deliverable is better than before the step. The previous levels are\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  saved as `final_levels_before7.json` in the tools folder if you want them back. A copy of the game is in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/steps/7/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
